          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The terms chiefly identify the humble, lowly, and dependent faithful—the anawim—who rely on God rather than wealth, status, power, or self-sufficiency and are promised divine deliverance and the kingdom.\",\"book_summary\":\"The terms identify people bowed and empty-handed before God, receptive to the gospel and kingdom because they are not self-sufficient.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Humble and needy people who depend upon God and receive his salvation.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Humble and empty-handed people who depend upon God and can receive the gospel and kingdom.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book emphasizes receptivity and contrasts the group with the rich or self-sufficient, while the consensus more explicitly acknowledges that the language can also denote literal socioeconomic poverty.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. The book restates the consensus’s primary spiritual-humility and dependence-on-God identification.\",\"rationale\":\"Both readings center on the same referent: lowly, humble, dependent people who trust God rather than themselves and consequently receive divine favor, the gospel, and the kingdom. The book’s imagery of being “bowed and empty-handed” is a concise formulation of the consensus concept of the anawim. Its contrast with the rich corresponds to the consensus contrast with wealth and self-sufficiency rather than replacing the referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Calling “Rich” the unqualified opposite may imply a sharper exclusion of materially wealthy people and may spiritualize passages about concrete poverty more strongly than the broader consensus permits. However, this does not materially alter the entry’s central identification.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
